GL: I think my father, he knew about it. But he wasn't directly involved. It was really my sister Ruth who kind of took charge of that. And in January of 1946, we began moving from DP camp to DP camp. The first one was right in Berlin. That was Zehlendorf. Then we moved to a DP camp near Munich called the Funk Caserne. And then eventually, we got to a DP camp just a bit north of Munich which was Indersdorf. And that was actually a Catholic cloister turned into a refugee spot for children. So it was a children's camp, which was much better for us. The first two were more adult camps and we should not have been there. And we'd lived there at this cloister for about six weeks, and then got on a train to Bremerhaven so that we could board ship to come to America.

INT: So from the time you were liberated until you started your journey to try to emigrate, what condition were you in after your two-year experience in hiding?

GL: [THINKING]

GL: I think...

I think we were happy. And we knew that we were going someplace else and that Papa would follow us in two weeks. So I don't think... And it turned out that he didn't come for ten years, but I don't think that... [THINKING] I mean, I think we felt that everything was going to be okay. We knew that we'd lost our mother, but we needed to move forward with life.

INT: Do you know what condition you and your siblings were in physically?

GL: Well, I know that we all had lice. [THROAT-SOUND] We were all very, very thin. The children's cloister, I was now at this point almost six and they thought I was three. So malnutrition. [PAUSES] Even, you know, the first thing that my adoptive parents did when I got here was to try to fatten me up because I was so tiny. They thought, in Germany, that I was... And there was food shortage, so trying to get nutrition was difficult. And during that two weeks, [GESTURING] when I mentioned that we came back to Berlin before liberation, my sister Gertrud wasn't part of that. She wasn't aware of what was going on because she had been hit by a truck, a mail truck or something. And so she was in hospital and... So she didn't know what was going on, but the rest of us, yeah, we were still being taken care of by whatever Jewish community was left.

INT: You said that you were advised to say that you were orphans. How could you do that if you still had a father?

GL: We just said we didn't have any family. [NODDING] We said we're seven children and we need to leave.

INT: When you were in the cloister, was that a nunnery?

GL: I guess so. Well, I mean, yeah, it was. Catholic sisters were taking care of us. It was a-- As I... I mean, I was going to say, "As I remember," but I don't really remember, except, yes, we were well fed and taken care of and given clothing and maybe deloused, I don't know.

INT: Do you remember how long you stayed there?

GL: About six weeks, in the cloister.

INT: Where did you go after that?

GL: We got on a train and went directly to Bremerhaven. And Bremerhaven was where we boarded the very first ship to leave Germany, postwar. In December of 1945, President Harry Truman decommissioned this transport ship. It was a troop transport ship called the SS Flasher. And we, along with 800 other people, were fortunate enough to be on that ship. To be able to come. And it was about a ten-day voyage, of which I remember... [LAUGHING] I remember lots of oranges and lots of seasickness.

And I remember arriving in New York and being-- Oh, and it-- I have to back up a tiny bit, in that we had lived through the war all together. And we got on the ship and they said, "Girls, go here, boys, go there." Well, we'd never been separated. And so, we said, "No. We got to get our brother in with us." So we sneaked him into our cabin so that we could continue to be together.

And when we arrived in New York, which was actually May 19th, 1946, we were told to go to sleep. "And in the morning, you have to get up very early because you're going to see something." So in the morning of May 20th, we laid at anchor in the harbor. And when we woke up, we were all told to come on deck and take a look. And what we saw was the Statue of Liberty. Well, it wasn't such a momentous thing for me because everybody had run over to the side of the ship, and I was afraid we were going to drown. [SMILING] I thought we would capsize. And of course, I didn't know what the Statue of Liberty was, but everyone else did, and we were thrilled.

INT: Were you required to have a sponsor in order to emigrate?

GL: Our sponsor was the Joint. And then, eventually, the Jewish Children's Bureau in Chicago because we said we wanted to go to Chicago. Why did we want to go to Chicago? Well, my brother Alfons heard of Al Capone, and he said, "Oh, that's where..." We had a choice of New York-- No, a choice of Miami, California or Chicago. So we came to Chicago. [SMILING] And the Jewish Children's Bureau then became our sponsor. So it was the Jewish Federation.

INT: Who greeted you when you arrived?

GL: I beg your pardon?

INT: Who greeted you? Was someone waiting for you?

GL: I guess social workers. I know we took the train. I don't know how long that train trip was, but I know that we then went to the offices of the JCB, where when we first docked in New York, I'm backing up again, in New York, we docked at pier 64, so we never went to Ellis Island, by the way. And we went into this enormous shed. There was a lot of, you know, waving, screaming, crying, because of the excitement of coming. And we had thousands of photos and pictures taken of us because it was unusual to have a family of seven children all intact. So we made all the headlines. I, of course, was scared of all the light bulbs flashing. Of all the cameras. But we seemed to... This was the first troop transport ship that arrived with refugees from the war, from Germany. And it was, you know, confusing but exciting time. We then lived in the Bronx for about a month, to get acclimated and for health reasons, we were quarantined there. And so, there were social workers that had to be part of either HIAS, or the Joint, that took care of us.

INT: What happened after you left the Bronx?

GL: Got on the-- Right from there, I mean, I remember that we went out to the store and bought shoes during one of our forays, and maybe more clothes or things. But then we got on the train and came directly to Chicago, to the Jewish Children's Bureau. Had more pictures taken. I have a photo of the six of us, or no, all seven of us. I'm sitting on the desk at the JCB and having some ice cream or something. And then, each of us were put in a taxicab and dropped off at a foster home, all on the south side, Jewish foster homes. Because who was going to take in seven children? And after I had a couple of not-happy experiences in a couple of foster homes, then I lived with one sister in her foster home, which was nice until my eventually adoptive parents came and visited and I lived with them.

INT: So, initially were you all separated into different foster homes?

GL: What was the question?

INT: Initially were you and your siblings all separated and placed in different foster homes?

GL: [NODDING]

GL: We were each placed in a separate foster home, but we all had contact with one another. And even when I was initially living with the Speigels, my adoptive parents, they would have the rest or some of my siblings come over so that I could spend time with them. But I had a lot of difficulty, kind of, not knowing where my loyalties should lie. And my siblings were saying, "Don't forget Mama, don't forget Papa." My adoptive parents were saying, "You're American," etcetera. So, in the eyes of the Jewish Children's Bureau, they felt that it would be in my best interest if I no longer had contact with my German siblings. It was just too hard for me. I mean, I was six and a half years old, and, you know, confused and didn't know what I was about. But I knew that these very nice people wanted me to live with them and take care of me, so that was fine with me, and so I agreed to the adoption. Whereas my sister Judith, who was three years older than I am, her foster parents wanted to adopt her and she said no. But she was nine. Perhaps almost ten.

INT: Do you know why she said no?

GL: Well, because we had a living father in Germany that we wanted to bring here.

INT: Were you in contact with your father at this time?

GL: I wasn't, but they were, especially my sister Judith. So she was instrumental in finally getting them. He, my father, his second wife, and their now two children to come to America. That didn't happen until 1956.

INT: Before your father came were your other siblings all but one adopted?

GL: I'm the only one that was adopted. Everyone else was in a foster home. My brother was 18, so the Jewish Children's Bureau actually were wonderful. They said, "You're 18. What do you want to do with your life? Where do you want to go to college?" And so they supported him and he went to college and became a physicist eventually.

And he was able... I don't think he lived with them for very long, the foster family. But they were lovely people, and he certainly kept in touch with them. And then he got married not long after that, and so did my sister Senta and Ruth. They were older. The younger ones, you know, Renee and Judy and I, needed to live with a family and have parents.

INT: During that time you said that you didn't have contact with your siblings, but did your siblings have contact with each other?

GL: My siblings all had contact with each other. I'm the only one who didn't. Which was very... [PAUSES 6 SECONDS]

It was... I mean, I knew I wanted to move on with my life, but there was guilt about not having contact with them. There was also guilt because I was now living a pretty privileged life. My father was a neurosurgeon. He was well known in Chicago and in the country. And I went to private... I started out at public school, but then went to a private school and then went to Northwestern University. And except for my brother who did go to IIT, Illinois Institute of Technology, although he was sorry. They had said, "You can go to the University of Chicago if you want to." But he, you know, was more interested in physics at that time. The rest of the family did not have any higher education. And through the years... there was a little bit of guilt-tripping done so that I... Excuse me. [DRINKING]

So that I wasn't sure I wanted to reconnect with this family. I had my own life. They had their own life. There was, however, one incident where... [THINKING] The incident where I was in a restaurant with my parents, my now brother and sister who were also adopted. It was a Chinese restaurant on 71st Street in Chicago. And as we came out of the restaurant, we were walking down the street, and we passed a family of four people, [GESTURING] a mother, a father, a boy and a girl. And as we passed each other, I kept staring at the man. And not knowing who he was, but staring at him, and turning around to look back at him as he turned around to look back at me. And it was my father. We never acknowledged each other. The...

It was a sort of surreal incidence.

I think, at about the same time, well, I would have been 16 at the time because that's when they came here, I was a dance student downtown. And one day somebody walked up to me on the street and said...

I'm not sure she said anything. She said, "Do you remember me?" And I started backing up and I said, "I don't remember your name, but I don't want to go back." It was my sister Ruth, who, unbeknownst to me, had gone up to the dance studio and spoken with the dance director, who immediately called my father, my adoptive father, told him that this young person had inquired about me and then waited for me till I came out. And she was told... Then he reported this to the Jewish Children's Bureau, who said to my sister Ruth, this is my sister Ruth, the one who arranged for us to come to America in the first place, that if she had any further contact with me, they were going to deport her and send her back to Germany.

That I had my own life. My father had given up his rights to me so that I could be adopted.

GL: And...

INT: To go back to that. Why did it take ten years for your father to come to the US?

GL: That's a very good question, and I don't know the answer.

INT: Did you know that he had remarried?

GL: I didn't know it, I don't think. But they, my sisters and brother, knew it.

INT: Were you corresponding with your father?

GL: I wasn't, but they, yes, they had had some slight correspondence. A little bit. Not much.

INT: Did your father marry a Jewish woman?

GL: No. My father married a woman who lived in the building. Apparently after we left, to start going from DP camp to... He was just... I mean, you know, he'd lost his wife. Now he didn't have his children, so he was really destroyed. And there was a woman who lived in the building, who kind of nursed him back to health, and this was the woman that he married. My siblings then began to call her, after learning about her, called her "Tante" because she wasn't their mother.

So there was some... And it was never a warm relationship, but it was there. But why it took ten years? [THINKING] Maybe he didn't have the money.

That's what I'm guessing.

INT: Did he remain Jewish?

GL: He did not remain Jewish. She was not Jewish. She actually had been married. Her husband had died. He was killed in the war. Her husband had been a soldier. And they were Catholic, so I think he... [PAUSES] The Germans had told my father early on that if he renounced his conversion, everything would be okay. And he would not renounce his conversion. He remained Jewish. But after we left, he then, I think, went back to... I don't know how religious he was, but he went back to being Catholic. And so they were raised, my step-sister and half-brother were raised as non-Jews.

INT: What do you remember about finally seeing your father after so many years and just passing each other without acknowledging one another.

GL: It was the strangest experience that I'd ever had. I knew I couldn't say anything. He knew he couldn't say anything. We had kind of given up our rights. I mean, I didn't, but he had to give up his rights so that I could be adopted. I knew if I said something, and I wasn't altogether sure that this was my father, but... [PAUSES] Had I said something, in my mind, it would have meant that I was disloyal to my adoptive family. And that was paramount in my thinking. These people had raised me for ten years or so, given me a loving home, did everything for me, and they were wonderful people.

So I felt that that's where my allegiance lay. I couldn't see the possibility at that age of having two relationships. I just-- It was-- I also, suddenly, you know, once I was adopted, I now had American cousins, and a new American family. My father had been from Canada, so there was a whole Canadian family. I mean, it was kind of... It was a little bit of how much can I take and how much can I give?

And I was probably a very selfish teenager who wanted a little bit of everything.

INT: Did you ever see each other again?

GL: Did I ever?

INT: Ever see each other again? You and your father?

GL: Never. [SHAKING HEAD]

INT: Or have any relationship with your half siblings?

GL: The-- As I-- [THINKING] In about 19-- Back up. Unbeknownst to me, several of my sisters always knew where I was and they would sometimes follow me. I went to the University of Chicago High School. They would follow behind me. They once came to the apartment building that we lived in. They were about to ring the doorbell and then they got chicken, so they didn't. My sister Ruth had, you know, seen me at the dance studio downtown. [PAUSES 5 SECONDS] They knew that my father was working at the University of Chicago.

So, it was almost... [SMILING] It was almost as though somebody was watching out for me. I had... And I always had this kind of feeling that there was somebody or something back there that was making sure that I was okay. In 1960, three days before I got married, I went to the pediatrician's office because you had to have a blood test. In those days, you had to have a blood test to make sure you didn't have venereal disease. I'm sitting in the pediatrician's waiting room and two women walked up to me and they said, "Are you Ginger?" "Yes." "Can we talk to you for a minute?" We went out and I said, "Of course." We went out into the ante room or something. And they said, "We're our sisters. We're here because we use the same pediatrician you use, and we're here for an appointment with our children. We saw your picture in the paper because you're getting married, and we wanted to wish you well, and to wish you a happy life." It was a very embarrassing exchange for me because I had not invited them to my wedding. I had not seen them that I could remember.

Except for, you know, seeing my sister Ruth. These were my sisters Gertrud and Renee that approached me.

INT: How many years had it been since you saw each other?

GL: Well, this was now 1960. I would say 15 years. I mean, I last saw them in 1946. All right. Then in 1963... [PAUSES 5 SECONDS] One question I always had when I started having children was they don't look like me. Everybody has siblings. I didn't look like Jonathan. I didn't look like Petra. No resemblance. I didn't look like my parents. And I got a phone call, 1963. And I'll never forget this. I was in my apartment, standing at the ironing board. [LAUGHING] Ironing God knows what. And the phone rang, I answered it, and she said, "Hi, this is your sister, Judith. I'm moving to Kansas next week. I don't know if I'll ever come back to Chicago. Would it be okay? I would love to see you." And I said, "Oh, yes. Absolutely." So we arranged for a few days from then she would come to see me. She came to the apartment, I opened the door, and it was the first time since 1946 that I saw someone who looked like me. And the flash of recognition was astounding. I said, "Judith, we're sisters. You know, we look alike." [LAUGHING] Because my children didn't look like me or that I could see. And then we had a lovely visit. And she did say, she did ask me if I wanted to reconnect with our father who was now living in Chicago. And I thought about it quite a bit. And I remember discussing it with my adoptive parents. I'm saying adoptive parents to distinguish between the birth and the adoptive. I consider them my parents. They're not adoptive to me. But for the purposes of this interview, I'm clarifying. I discussed it with my parents. And my father said, "If you would like to make contact with your father, please feel free to do so. But know this. You can't just see him once out of curiosity. If you make contact, you will have to establish a relationship because it would not be fair to him." [PAUSES] And so, after discussing it with my husband, and I decided I would not, I just couldn't. Couldn't do it. Because even though my father said, "Go ahead." I still felt that it would be a betrayal.

INT: Before we hear more about your relationship with your siblings and your father here, I just want to go back to the time when you emigrated here. So, you didn't speak the language. Were you put into school immediately?

GL: I went... Yes, the first school that I remember going to was Shakespeare Elementary School where all I remember is that everybody made fun of me. I had a strong German accent. I still probably had my German name. Hadn't been changed yet. [THINKING] And all I knew was that Germany, anything German was reprehensible. They were the bad people. And I wanted nothing to do with Germany. So my language, my accent, maybe my braids,

I don't know, but anything that would remind me of Germany was verboten to me. I didn't want anything to do with it. So I worked very hard to learn English very quickly, which for a six or seven-year-old child is easy to do. But I forgot my German. Because, I mean, my parents and I spoke Yiddish to each other a tiny bit, or I spoke German, they spoke Yiddish so that we could understand each other. After a very short time, I didn't need to. I spoke a sort of a pidgin English, and we communicated that way. [PAUSES] And I didn't-- Whatever customs I had had, I no longer had. It was very important that I become truly Americanized. I didn't want anything that smacked of foreignness.

INT: Were you aware if there were other children that had also emigrated here?

GL: That also had come here. There was, yes, a girl in grammar school. Her name was [SMILING] Many years later, she was Hannah Weiss, and we got together, it was just a wonderful meeting. [?Hannah Kasten?] did not come here until 1948 with her grandmother, and she lived in Berlin the entire time. [PAUSES] I think her parents had died. I can't recall.

So we had a wonderful reunion. So, yes, I did know that there were others.

INT: Once you made friends, did you talk to them at all about your experiences before coming here?

GL: Well, I must have with Hannah, but perhaps superficially. And I didn't talk much about-- It was something we didn't talk about. I didn't even talk about it with my children. I didn't even talk about it much with my husband. He kept saying, "Well, don't you remember things from when you were two years old and three?" I said, "No, who could remember back that far?" He has a fantastic memory, and he remembered. So he thought I should, but no. A very interesting side note. I mentioned that I lost my German completely. Completely. Don't speak it. Can't pronounce it. Don't read it. Nothing.

In 1984, I had an accident and I was in the hospital. That's why I use a wheelchair. So I was in the hospital for several months. And I was having a very difficult time. I was hallucinating a lot, and I was on morphine for a while, so some heavy, heavy drugs.

And I dreamt in German.

The language came right back.

Today I speak no German. I can't tell you what anything German looks like. But it's in the brain. It's back there. It's sort of in the side, but it's there. You never forget.

INT: So at that time, you're learning to speak English, you're making friends. What were your goals?

GL: To be a dancer. My goal in life was to be a dancer. That's all I ever wanted.

INT: How did you get interested in dance?

GL: [LAUGHING]

GL: I was dancing on the ship coming over, on the captain's table. This little kid entertaining the other passengers. So I just always, I was always very, very active. [GESTURING]

And very athletic, very well coordinated. And then my mother, of course, took me to dance class as all mothers take little girls to dance class. And then I was fortunate enough that they sent me to summer camp at Interlochen, Michigan, national music camp. And the dance teacher there said, "I've got the teacher for you in Chicago. There's only one person, Edna McRae. You take her there." And that's what my mother did once I came back. And so I studied at the McRae studio for many years.

INT: You mentioned the name of the first school that you attended here.

GL: [NODDING] The first school was Shakespeare Elementary School, and I think I went there with my sister Renee. Although there may have been another school for a week or so, I can't remember. But then after I moved to the Speigels, I then went, or maybe even with the Speigels, I went to Shakespeare because of where we lived. We lived on Drexel Boulevard. Once we moved to Madison Park in Hyde Park, I went to Kenwood Elementary School and I went there, and that's where [?Hannah Kasten?] was. And I went there until sort of seven and a half. Seventh grade, but seven and a half, almost eighth grade, when I was admitted to the University of Chicago Laboratory Schools. U-High. But living in Hyde Park, of course, we belonged to Sinai Temple, which was a very large temple on the south side that was mostly populated by German Jews. And the rabbi, Louis Mann, lived not too far from us, and we had Sunday school. So every Sunday morning, he would pick me up in his car and drive me to Sunday school. He was also the rabbi who married us. But he was... Why am I bringing him up? Oh, I know why. Because in 1951, they had just finished building the new temple on South Shore Boulevard. And so they were having a dedication. And I wrote, that is my mother wrote remarks and they liked them. And so I got to speak at the dedication. [PAUSES] And I talked about how proud I was to be Jewish and American and to be able to tell the policeman on the corner something. I mean, I was, you know, 11 years old. That I was American and coming to Sunday school and allowed to practice Judaism. That was-- And I have photos from that time and all.

INT: Where did you go after high school?

GL: I went to Northwestern University and majored in theater and dance. Mostly theater because they didn't have a very good dance department at that time. [LAUGHING]

INT: Did you get married?

GL: And then I got married. 1960. So after my junior year. And we lived on the north side.

INT: What was your husband's name?

GL: My husband's name was Fred, Frederic [?Stanton?] Lane. And we... Fourteen months after we got married, our son Russel was born. And then 1963, Beth was born, our daughter. And in 1969, our daughter Jennifer was born. So I have three children and seven grandchildren.

INT: How did you meet your husband?

GL: I met my husband on a blind date. Oh, my gosh. Just... [SMILING] At that time, the Theater Guild was, I guess it was the Theater Guild, was a functioning organization. And so this blind date, this man, young guy called me up, and, "My parents have given me tickets to the theater. Would you like to go?" I said, "Sure."

I was a freshman at Northwestern. [PAUSES 5 SECONDS] We went to see "The Diary of Anne Frank." [PAUSES] Which, of course, [LAUGHING] my poor husband didn't know what he was taking me to.

So that was quite an event itself. I also got to meet at Northwestern Joseph Schildkraut, he was an actor who portrayed Otto Frank when "The Diary of Anne Frank" came to Chicago in about 1958 or so.

INT: How did you choose your children's names?

GL: Well, my children's names, I was told, I think, mostly by my husband, Fred, that we, you know, we should name our children after a relative who had deceased. And he had either his grandmother, Rebecca, had died. And so I said, "But it's a boy." That's how we got Russel. Beth, I don't remember at all. And Jennifer, I don't remember how we got their names.

INT: You mentioned that you really didn't discuss your experiences with your husband.

GL: Well, I mean, certainly he knew, but I didn't go into exquisite detail.

INT: When did you tell your children that you were a Holocaust survivor?

GL: I never said that. I never told them I was a Holocaust survivor. They knew, of course, that I was from Germany. I told them that. Again, I would say more of the bare outlines. I didn't really acknowledge that I was a Holocaust survivor. Until recently, I didn't think I deserved that label because I had not been in concentration camp. I had not been tortured or suffered abuse. So how could I stand with those who did.

But of course, after doing much reading, I mean, yes, in fact, I was, I am a survivor,

But I never wanted to go back to Germany until 2017, after my brother died, and I felt I need to go and I need to take my children. By that time I was divorced, but my former husband Fred came with us as well and we all went back to Germany. And walked around the Scheunenviertel which I didn't remember at all. But... And then went to the Jewish Museum of Berlin and, you know, Checkpoint Charlie. So we did a tiny bit of the touristy things. But the next day-- I had been in contact with these people who lived in Worin in the village where we were living for two years. And after a bit of correspondence back and forth, we planned to go and visit them. So we hired a car and we drove out to Worin, excuse me, and had a wonderful time with them. They just opened their hearts and their home to us. It turns out that he was the village historian. Excuse me. [GESTURING]

INT: Who are you referring to?

GL: Herbert. [PAUSES 5 SECONDS] They lived, in 2017, on this little-- They had a home just outside of... In the village of Worin where we'd been hidden. He'd been, I think, a high school teacher or a town historian. They knew about us. My brother had visited with them. So we visited with them. And I thought we would just meet these two people. And there was another man there. And who was that man? It was Arthur Schmidt's grandson who had not known what his grandfather had done to save us.

Fast forward seven months. [PAUSES] And now Arthur Schmidt III, and his wife and their son, Arthur Schmidt, they're all named Arthur Schmidt, met us in Jerusalem, and we went to Yad Vashem to have Paula and Arthur Schmidt acknowledged, and a plaque at Yad Vashem. So we were there for the ceremony, which was a wonderfully moving experience.

INT: As Righteous Among the Nations?

GL: [NODDING] They were named as Righteous Among the Nations.

INT: His grandfather had never told him about saving your family?

GL: [SHAKING HEAD]

GL: Never. That's the kind of person he was.

INT: When you went back, did you go to the apartment that you were born in?

GL: It was no longer there. They had torn it down and they even changed the name of the street from Dragonerstrasse which is what I remember, to Max-Beer-Strasse. But we went to the building. I mean, we went to the same place. And we walked around, we didn't go inside, but we went into the kind of courtyard [GESTURING] in the back of the building, and it was very interesting just to be in that neighborhood.

INT: You mentioned that you had three children. Do you have any grandchildren?

GL: I have seven. Very happy to say I have seven grandchildren. My son Russy has a daughter, Taylor, who is a dancer. The only other dancer in the family. And son, Trevor. My daughter Beth has three boys, two of whom are twins. That's Joshua, named Joshua because my father had just died in May and Josh was born at the end of July. So he's Josh. And then two years later or three years later, they had twin boys, Sebastian and Oliver. And so those are those three, five. And then my daughter Jennifer and her husband Mark, have two children. Elliot, who is now 21 or will be 21. He's there. And Anya who's 19 and is a freshman. They're both at Georgetown University in D.C. So I think that counts for seven.

INT: Did your children marry other Jewish people?

GL: No. None of my children married Jewish people.

INT: Did you raise them--

GL: But they've all raised their children Jewish.

INT: Did you raise them with Jewish traditions?

